hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1119","**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Khalaf al-'Asqalani, narrated to us Adam (i.e., ibn Abi Iyas), narrated to us Qays (i.e., ibn ar-Rabi'), narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abi Layla, from Dawud ibn 'Ali, from his father, from Ibn 'Abbas (RA), who said:

Al-'Abbas (RA) sent me to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). I came to him in the evening, and he was in the house of my maternal aunt Maimunah bint al-Harith. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood up to pray during the night. When he had prayed the two rak'ahs of Fajr, he said:

*""O Allah, I ask You for mercy from You with which You guide my heart, gather my affairs, unite my scattered matters, return what has gone astray, rectify my religion, protect my absent ones, elevate my present ones, purify my deeds, whiten my face, inspire my righteousness, and protect me with it from all evil. O Allah, grant me sincere faith, certainty that leaves no room for disbelief, and mercy by which I may attain the honor of Your nearness in this world and the Hereafter. O Allah, I ask You for success at the time of judgment, the lodging of the martyrs, the life of the blessed, the companionship of the Prophets, and victory over the enemies.*

*O Allah, I place my need before You, even if my understanding falls short, my deeds are weak, and I am in need of Your mercy. So I ask You, O Granter of all matters, O Healer of the chests, just as You protect the seas, protect me from the punishment of the Fire, from the call of the Trumpet, and from the trials of the graves.*

*O Allah, whatever my understanding falls short of, my deeds are too weak for, and my intention does not reach—any good You have promised to one of Your servants or any good You grant to any of Your creation—I ask You for it, and I turn to You, O Lord of the worlds.*

*O Allah, make us guides who are rightly guided, neither astray nor causing others to stray, at war with Your enemies and at peace with Your allies. We love by Your love the people and oppose by Your enmity those who oppose You.*

*O Allah, this is my supplication, and upon You is the response. Or: the answer—(here) Ibn Khalaf expressed doubt. This is my effort, and upon You is the reliance. There is no power and no strength except with Allah.*

*O Allah, O Possessor of the firm rope and the right command, I ask You for security on the Day of Reckoning, and for Paradise on the Day of Eternity with the near ones, the bowing and prostrating ones, the fulfillers of covenants. Indeed, You are the Most Merciful, the Most Loving, and You do what You will.*

*Glory be to He Who wraps Himself in might and speaks with it. Glory be to He Who adorns Himself with glory and honors Himself thereby. Glory be to He Who is not fit to be praised except by Him. Glory be to He Who has enumerated all things and knows them. Glory be to He of bounty and favors. Glory be to He of power and generosity.*

*O Allah, grant me light in my heart, light in my grave, light in my hearing, light in my sight, light in my hair, light in my skin, light in my flesh, light in my blood, light in my bones, light before me, light behind me, light to my right, light to my left, light above me, and light below me. O Allah, increase me in light, grant me light, and make for me light.""*"
